<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter Kenilorea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1943">1943</a>–<a href="/years/2016">2016</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Peter Kenilorea was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-islands">Solomon Islands</a> teacher and politician who became chief minister in 1976 and the first prime minister when the islands became independent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> on 7 July 1978. He served as prime minister again in the mid-1980s and later as speaker of the national parliament.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-kenilorea.jpg" alt="Peter Kenilorea" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jenny Scott from Adelaide, Australia · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Kenilorea_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kenilorea was born on 23 May 1943 in Takataka village on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaita">Malaita</a>, of ꞌAreꞌare ethnicity. After school in Malaita and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honiara">Honiara</a> he won a scholarship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, where he attended Wesley College, Whanganui Collegiate School and Ardmore College, training as a teacher at Ardmore. He then taught in Malaita and co-founded the Solomon Islands Christian Association. A founding member of the Solomon Islands United Party, he lost the ꞌAreꞌare seat to David Kausimae in 1973 but won East ꞌAreꞌare in 1976, when the six other potential candidates withdrew for him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>As chief minister he was a leading figure of the independence movement, criticising British rule for failing to advance the country economically and for building only one school, and he took part in the negotiations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Prime minister at 35 in a society that associated leadership with elders, he held that traditional leadership could live on among the people while national leadership should rest with the well educated. After the 1980 election the United Party's 16 seats and the 15 of the Independent Group kept him in office, but a motion of no confidence in October 1981 brought in Solomon Mamaloni.</p><p>Returning after the 1984 election with 21 members of parliament behind him against 13 for Mamaloni, Kenilorea organised the country's provincial system of government and passed tribal land rights into law. He resigned in 1986 after being implicated in a financial scandal and was succeeded by his deputy, Ezekiel Alebua. He later served as Minister of Foreign Affairs in 1988-1989 and 1990-1993.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During the conflict between the Malaita Eagle Force and the Isatabu Freedom Movement he co-chaired the peace talks of 2000 with Paul Tovua, respected by both groups, and then chaired the Peace Monitoring Council. He was Speaker of Parliament from 2001 to 2010. He died on 24 February 2016 after a period of illness; his body lay in state in the parliament, and his funeral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honiara">Honiara</a> on 1 March was a national day of mourning. He is recognised as the founding father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-islands">Solomon Islands</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peter Kenilorea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A legislative council and self-government followed, and the islands became independent as Solomon Islands on 7 July 1978, with <b>Peter Kenilorea</b> as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-solomon-islands-protectorate">British Solomon Islands Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>The administration was renamed the Solomon Islands in 1975, self-government came in 1976, and independence on 7 July 1978 under Prime Minister <b>Peter Kenilorea</b>, with Elizabeth II as queen.</blockquote><a href="/state/solomon-islands">Solomon Islands</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaita">Malaita</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honiara">Honiara</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-9,161&amp;year=1990" aria-label="Malaita on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Peter Kenilorea"><image href="/maps/globes/g15000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5923 5767h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5923 5767h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-9,161&amp;year=1990">What was here in 1990? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
